Why DL?
	- ML's prediction is limited and can only handle structured.
	- With ML, the image classification, test analysis, speech recognitions etc cannot be performed.

How DL?
	- through the human way of learning like learning in batches, learning rate and repeated learning (epochs).
	- this human way of learning intelligence is called Artificial Neural Network.
	- the ANN cosists of I/P layer, hidden layers and O/P layer
	- the data is fed into this ANN in the I/P layer and the O/P layer gives the prediction. -- this is called forward propagation.
	- the difference in the output predicted and the actual is the error or loss.
	- the model during the training phase, constantly updates the weights and biases of the hidden layer and the output layer.
	- based on the loss calculated the weights are updated from output layer to hidden layers. -- this is called backward propagation.

Building Blocks of ANN:
	- Batches 
		-> a data of 10000 will be splitted into small batches. 
		-> in order to prevent the too much memory consumption by model for training.
	- Epochs
		-> number of iterations to be performed for learinig the data.
		-> one iteration consits of 1 forward propagation + 1 backward propagation - applied on all the batches
		-> example 10000 data. 100 batches. 1 epoch = 100FP + 100 BP
		-> this also depends on the type of batching technique used.
	- Activation functions
		-> to introduce non-linearity in the neural network
		-> enable the neuron/node to decide the information is relevant or not
			|- Sigmoid
			|- TanH
			|- ReLU
			|- Leaky ReLU
			|- Softmax
	- Loss functions
		-> the difference between the predicted output and the actual output is calculated using different functions called loss functions.
		-> the goal is to have the loss value minimum.
	- optimizers
		-> to reduce the loss, the model weights are updated in the backward propagation
		-> during this optimization, the vanishing gradient and the exploding gradient problems occur.
			|-- when the gradient (updates) are too small, the vanishing gradient problem occurs.
			|-- the early layers get zero updates and stops learning
			|-- when the gradient is very large, the exploding gradient occurs.
			|-- happens due to poor weight initialization, and results in unstable learning of the model due to oscillating loss
	- Learning Rate
		-> a small positive value that controls how much the model weights are to be updated.
		-> if rate is high, then the model will have fast learinig but will be unstable due to overshooting of optimal minimum
	
	
